  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nToday - Tonight: Periods of Light Rain\n\nA stacked mid to upper tropospheric closed low meanders ESE \nacross northern Iowa this afternoon and tonight, bringing with \nit periods of light to moderate showers. With the showers driven\nmainly by DCVA ahead of the low and little in the way of deeper\nmoisture and mesoscale ascent, rainfall rates will be on the \nlighter side. As of 11 am, rainfall amounts around the I-35 \ncorridor had topped out around 1/2 of an inch, but this band \nwas starting to thin and weaken. While total rainfall amounts \nsouth of I-90 could approach similar values (20-30% chance), \nthis rain will fall over the course of several hours and have \nlittle impact and any elevated river levels. The rainfall axis \ngradually spreads ESE ahead of the low centroid, persisting \nthrough the evening hours before departing to the southeast by \nsunrise on Tuesday. With the widespread clouds and rain today, \nhighs will only top out in the low to mid 60s, a full ten \ndegreed below average. \n\n\nRest of the Work Week: Cool and Mostly Dry\n\nThe region resides along the western periphery of a large \nsouthern Ontario/Quebec high pressure cell throughout the \nbalance of the work week, keeping a cool and dry east/southeast \nfetch in place that will limit the intrusion of any rain into \nthe region. A subtle shortwave grazes the Dakotas on Wednesday, \nwith convection likely to move at least into central Minnesota \nby Thursday morning. However, how this rain shield evolves \nafterwards is a source of forecast uncertainty. Most solutions \ndecay this system relatively quickly before reaching the \nMississippi River, but there are a few outlier solutions (10%) \nthat advect the rain farther east. However, odds are favoring \nmore of a dry forecast during this period. \n\n\nUpcoming Weekend: Next Chance of Rain\n\nA deeper longwave trough approaches the region for the weekend,\nshunting the ridge to the east and opening the door for the \nreturn of rainfall. While the bulk of the medium to longer range\nguidance have this wave resolved, timing and placement of this \ntrough make it difficult to ascertain any details about this \nrainfall. What is more certain is that given the lack of deeper \nmoisture and progressive nature of the surface front, rainfall \namounts do not look to be overly high with LREF rainfall \nprobabilities of >1/2\" being around 10-20%.",
